WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:03.180
LEIGHA JARETT: Just like a
Ferrari, every part of BigQuery

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.270
was designed to make
your queries run fast.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:09.090
And that includes
BigQuery's managed storage.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.520
In today's episode, we're
going under the hood

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.240
to figure out exactly how
BigQuery stores this data so

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.240
that you can make informed
decisions on how to optimize

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.074
your BigQuery storage.

00:00:20.074 --> 00:00:23.462
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.300
When talking about
BigQuery storage,

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.590
there are a few things I
always like to point out.

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:35.980
First off, BigQuery offers
fully managed storage,

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:39.010
meaning you don't have to worry
about provisioning servers

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:41.890
or worry about
configuring any hardware.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:44.830
All sizing is done
automatically and you only

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:46.900
pay for what you use.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:51.070
Next, BigQuery uses what
we call columnar datastore.

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:54.130
Taking a step back for a
moment, traditional relational

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:57.310
databases, like
Postgres and MySQL,

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:01.780
store data row by row in
record-oriented storage.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:04.840
This makes them really good
at transactional updates,

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:07.480
for example, changing
the status of an order

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.120
because they only need
to open up a single row

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.320
to read or write data.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.630
However, if you want to perform
an aggregation like summing up

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:19.510
the Sale Price column to
get your total revenue,

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:23.080
you would need to read the
entire table into memory.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:26.380
BigQuery, on the other
hand, uses columnar storage,

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:30.340
where data is co-located
by column rather than row.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:33.670
This makes BigQuery an ideal
solution for analytical use

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:35.470
cases.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.690
Now, to calculate
the total revenue,

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:41.110
BigQuery will only need to
read in the Sale Price column.

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:45.400
Internally, BigQuery stores data
in a proprietary column format

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.410
called Capacitor.

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:49.630
One really cool
thing about Capacitor

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:52.990
is that it uses relevant
factors like the type of data

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:56.980
and how the data is being used
to reshuffle rows and encode

00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:58.360
columns.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:01.840
One example is
run-length encoding.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.450
Let's pretend that the
value 1 appears three

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:06.880
times in a row in your data.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:10.150
Rather than storing
1, 1, 1, Capacitor

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:13.150
can instead store 1:3.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:15.100
But what if the rows
are ordered such

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:18.580
that there are no long
runs of the same value?

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:20.710
Well, to solve this,
Capacitor simply

00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:25.330
reorders the rows to
obtain a compact encoding.

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:28.480
So now we know about
BigQuery's file format.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.810
But where do these files
actually get saved?

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:33.730
The persistence
layer for BigQuery

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:37.720
is provided by the distributed
file system throughout Google--

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:39.430
Colossus.

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:43.540
Colossus has a ton of disks
that live on a ton of servers.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.730
But when you have
so many, chances

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:49.580
are that dozens of disks
are going to fail each day.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:53.890
So the goal is to make
sure that no data is lost.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:56.860
Colossus ensures durability
by using something

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:01.030
called erasure encoding, which
breaks data into fragments

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:03.130
and saves redundant
pieces across

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:05.500
a set of different disks.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:09.970
However, to ensure the data
is both durable and available,

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:14.020
the data is also replicated
to another availability zone

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:16.540
within the same region
that was designated

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:19.610
when you created your data set.

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:22.810
This means data is saved in a
bunch of different buildings

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:26.470
that has a different
power system and network.

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:29.200
The chances of multiple
availability zones going

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:32.650
offline at once is very small.

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:35.410
But if you use
multi-region locations,

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:40.210
like the US or the EU,
BigQuery stores another copy

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:43.750
of the data in an
off-region replica.

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:46.420
That way, the data is
recoverable in the event

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:49.300
of a major disaster.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.760
Aside from replication,
Colossus also

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:53.710
protects your data
by ensuring that it

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:57.610
is 100% encrypted at rest.

00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:00.640
Plus, if you want to
control encryption yourself,

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.570
you can use customer-managed
encryption keys.

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:06.550
And the great thing
about all of this

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:09.850
is that encoding,
encryption and replication

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:12.650
are included in the price
of BigQuery storage.

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:15.140
So no hidden costs.

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:17.170
So far, we've talked
about the format

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:19.600
in which BigQuery stores data.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:21.970
And we've talked about
the physical file system

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:24.350
where the data is stored.

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:26.170
But how do we
ensure that data is

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:29.170
stored in a way that makes
our queries as performant

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:31.360
as possible?

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:35.170
BigQuery has a storage optimizer
that helps arrange data

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:37.780
into the optimal
shape for querying

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:41.020
by periodically rewriting files.

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.360
Files may be written
first in a format that

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:44.890
is fast to write to.

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:47.080
But later, BigQuery
will format them

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:50.410
in a way that's fast to query.

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:53.590
Aside from the optimization
happening behind the scenes,

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:55.720
there are also a few
things that you can

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:58.780
do to further enhance storage.

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:02.170
First off, BigQuery allows
you to partition your table

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:04.120
when it's created.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:07.720
Partitioning basically allows
you to divide large tables

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:10.690
into smaller chunks
or partitions, which

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:14.320
are stored in a physically
separate location.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:17.140
When you include a "where"
clause in your query,

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:19.660
BigQuery will only need
to read in the partitions

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:22.420
that you actually need.

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:25.540
BigQuery allows you to
partition data based on the time

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:26.890
it was ingested--

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:30.820
a date column with
granularity as low as hourly,

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:33.340
or an integer column.

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:35.260
When deciding on
partitions, you should

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:37.990
consider how people
at your organization

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:40.220
are using this data.

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:43.390
For example, if data
consumers are most often

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:46.180
looking at orders
placed in the past week,

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:49.820
it might make sense to partition
your table by order date

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:52.360
so that you can
efficiently read data just

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:54.910
from the past seven days.

00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:56.950
Partitions are
designed for places

00:05:56.950 --> 00:05:59.170
where there is a
large amount of data

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:02.200
and a low number
of distinct values.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.840
A good rule of thumb is
making sure partitions

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:07.990
are greater than 1 gigabyte.

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:09.940
If you over partition
your tables,

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:12.340
you'll create a lot
of metadata, which

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.500
means that reading
in lots of partitions

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:17.870
may actually slow
down your query.

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:21.370
If you need higher cardinality
or you have a smaller table,

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:23.960
you should use clustering.

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:25.960
Clustering is like
sorting your tables

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:28.210
on a particular set of columns.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:31.270
For example, clustering
our table by customer

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:35.350
ID would sort the rows
based on the customer ID.

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:37.390
In BigQuery, you're
able to choose up

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:40.120
to four columns to cluster on.

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:41.950
This allows for
efficient lookouts

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:44.650
because the query engine
needs to open files

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:46.690
that have the cluster key.

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:49.210
For example, if
we query our table

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:51.370
and include a
filter to only look

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:54.130
at orders placed by
customer number one,

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:57.340
then we only need to
open up this file.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:00.340
The tricky part of clustering
is maintaining the clusters

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:02.350
when the data is changing.

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:04.990
But BigQuery solves
this by periodically

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:07.240
reclustering each table.

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:10.630
This happens automatically
without any user intervention

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:13.180
or additional cost.

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:16.690
Partitioning and clustering
are often used together.

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:20.410
We can partition by the low
cardinality field, for example,

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:22.510
the created_at
date of the order,

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:25.060
and cluster by the
high cardinality one,

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:27.250
like customer_id.

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:30.740
This lets you operate over a
date range slice of the table,

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:32.620
but it also lets
you find records

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:35.620
from a particular customer
without having to scan

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:38.500
all the data in the partition.

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:40.600
As with partitioning,
you should always

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:42.610
try and figure out
the types of questions

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:46.240
users will be asking of the data
to decide the keys that you'll

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:48.280
use to cluster.

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:51.470
Want to learn more about
storage optimization?

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:53.720
Check out the links below.

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:55.480
Thanks for tuning
into this episode

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:56.860
